# Functional test: an unrelated commit while an IPsec tunnel carries traffic.
# VALIDATES: spec-fixit-ipsec-peer-reload-ignored AC-2 -- a reload that changes no member
#            of a running peer restarts nothing, and the kernel keeps the states and the
#            policies the tunnel installed.
# PREVENTS: R-1, which is the way the fix for AC-1 can overshoot. peerConfigChanged now
#           compares the WHOLE ipsec.SiteToSitePeer value rather than eight named members
#           (ipsec.SiteToSitePeer.Equal), so a member that differed between two parses of
#           one unchanged file would restart every peer on every commit. A tunnel that
#           bounces whenever an operator commits anything is a worse defect than the one
#           the totality removes.
#
# WHAT MAKES THE COMMIT UNRELATED. peer-a's `peer peer-b` block is byte-identical in the
# two config files. The second file adds a SEPARATE peer, peer-quiet, which responds on
# 127.0.0.3 and which nothing ever contacts. So the reload has real work to do and none of
# it is on the running session.
#
# THE TWO HALVES OF THE CLAIM, and why neither alone is enough:
#   - reject=stderr ike: stopping peer proves nothing was restarted. That string is
#     logged in exactly one place, the removal loop of reconcilePeers, and shutdown does
#     not log it.
#   - expect=stderr peer-quiet proves the reload was APPLIED. Without it a SIGHUP that
#     reached nobody would pass this test by changing nothing at all.
# The driver adds the kernel half: the ESP SPIs and the tunnel's policies are read before
# the commit and re-read across a settle window, and a restart removes both synchronously
# inside the OnConfigure handler, so a bounce cannot hide inside that window.
#
# WHY peer-a IS THE DAEMON THIS TEST RELOADS, AND WHY ITS FILE IS ze-bgp.conf. Only a
# command whose binary is literally `ze` gets its `-` replaced by a config FILE and gets a
# daemon.pid written for it (zeReadyFileEnabled and the substitution loop in
# runner_exec.go both test `binName == "ze"`). peer-b launches through `env` to set its own
# dataplane, so its binary is `env`, its config is PIPED to stdin, and it publishes no pid.
# peer-a is therefore the only daemon with a file to rewrite and a pid to signal, and its
# file takes the default name ze-bgp.conf because it is the only block that reaches
# zeConfigFileName (runner_config.go).
#
# WHY ONE DATAPLANE AND NOT TWO. The kernel keys an XFRM state on (src, dst, spi, proto)
# and the two ends of one negotiated Child SA are the SAME two states, so a second
# XfrmStateAdd answers EEXIST. peer-a therefore runs the real xfrm backend and peer-b the
# noop one, and every kernel assertion below is about peer-a.
#
# WHY THESE PREFIXES. The policies are real and node-wide for the life of the test, so
# they name RFC 5737 documentation addresses no traffic can be using. They are halves of
# TEST-NET-3, distinct as printed strings from the TEST-NET-1 halves and quarters
# ipsec-child-rekey-xfrm.ci and ipsec-child-rekey-xfrm-narrowing.ci install, from the
# TEST-NET-2 halves ipsec-peer-reload-applies-selectors.ci installs, and from the
# TEST-NET-2/3 /24 pair ipsec-teardown-leaves-nothing.ci installs.
#
# Gated option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin: peer-a programs real XFRM state, so this SKIPs
# on a host without CAP_NET_ADMIN (the unprivileged dev box) and runs as root in the QEMU
# VM, where `fsuite ipsec` in internal/le/qemu/alltests.go executes the suite.

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
# Serialize against every other real-XFRM ipsec test. `ip xfrm state` and
# `ip xfrm policy` are NODE-WIDE, so a sibling running concurrently adds SPIs and
# policies this test's readers cannot tell from its own.
option=exclusive:group=ipsec-xfrm
option=env:var=ze_test_ike_port:value=$PORT2
option=timeout:value=120s
option=env:var=ze_log_ike:value=info


# peer-a's config AFTER the unrelated commit. The `peer peer-b` block is byte-identical
# to the peer-a block below; peer-quiet is the whole difference.
tmpfs=config2.conf:terminator=EOF_CONFIG2
vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer peer-b {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type initiate
				local-address 127.0.0.1
				remote-address 127.0.0.2
				mode tunnel
				traffic-selector 1 {
					protocol 6
					local {
						prefix 203.0.113.0/25
						port 179
					}
					remote {
						prefix 203.0.113.128/25
					}
				}
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
			peer peer-quiet {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type respond
				local-address 127.0.0.3
				remote-address 127.0.0.4
				mode tunnel
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-5678
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONFIG2

# ── peer-b (background, noop dataplane): binds 127.0.0.2, never reloads ──
stdin=peer-b:terminator=EOF_PEER_B
vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer peer-a {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type respond
				local-address 127.0.0.2
				remote-address 127.0.0.1
				mode tunnel
				traffic-selector 1 {
					protocol 6
					local {
						prefix 203.0.113.128/25
					}
					remote {
						prefix 203.0.113.0/25
						port 179
					}
				}
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_PEER_B

# ── peer-a (background, REAL xfrm): binds 127.0.0.1, and is the daemon reloaded ──
stdin=peer-a:terminator=EOF_PEER_A
vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer peer-b {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type initiate
				local-address 127.0.0.1
				remote-address 127.0.0.2
				mode tunnel
				traffic-selector 1 {
					protocol 6
					local {
						prefix 203.0.113.0/25
						port 179
					}
					remote {
						prefix 203.0.113.128/25
					}
				}
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_PEER_A

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=env ze_test_ike_dataplane=noop ze -:stdin=peer-b:name=peer-b
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=peer-a:name=peer-a
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze-test fixture ipsec/peer-reload-leaves-tunnel-alone:timeout=110s
# signal=term, not the default kill: peer-a's policies are REAL and node-wide, and the
# release that removes them runs on the way out of runEngine. A SIGKILLed peer-a leaves
# them in the kernel for the rest of the suite, where ipsec-teardown-leaves-nothing.ci
# asserts the XFRM tables are empty.
cmd=stop:seq=4:name=peer-a:signal=term
cmd=stop:seq=5:name=peer-b:signal=term

expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=INSTALLED:
expect=stdout:contains=RELOADED:
expect=stdout:contains=STABLE:
expect=stdout:contains=POLICY-STABLE:
# The reload really happened: peer-quiet exists only in the second config file, and
# reconcilePeers logs the peer name when it starts the session for it.
expect=stderr:contains=peer-quiet
# Nothing was restarted. reconcilePeers' removal loop is the only producer of this line.
reject=stderr:pattern=ike: stopping peer
reject=stdout:pattern=BOUNCED
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
